  "name": "European Townhouse",
  "category": "Classical",
  "blurb": "Six storeys of stone with a mansard crown and scattered balconies.",
  "teach": "The nineteenth-century apartment block is a vertical rhythm held together by horizontals: a shopfront at the pavement, five near-identical storeys of tall narrow windows, a string course marking every floor, and a heavy cornice under a mansard roof. The mansard's break is a fraction of how far the plan can erode at all, not of its width, so it lands in the same place on a small block and a large one - and the PITCH, not the break, is what sets how tall the crown reads. The balconies are set to Scattered rather than to every window: a seeded share, which is what stops a long terrace looking like a spreadsheet. Re-roll the seed and they move.",
